> I want to use re in python to either find things of the form:
> ```Some text.```
> Or
> ```python
> Some text.```
> 
> How can I do this, and always just return the "Some text." part?
**assistant**:
> To achieve this, you can use Python's `re` module to define a regular expression pattern that captures the text inside the backticks. Here, we aim to match both single and triple backtick-enclosed phrases and extract the desired content. Here's how you can do it:
> 
> ```python
> import re
> 
> # Your input string
> text = """
> Here is some text:
> ```Some text.```
> And more:
> ```python
> Some Python text.```
> """
> 
> # Regular expression pattern
> pattern = r'```(?:python\s+)?(.*?)```'
> 
> # Find all matches in the text
> matches = re.findall(pattern, text, re.DOTALL)
> 
> for match in matches:
>     print("Captured text: ", match.strip())
> ```
> 
> ### Explanation:
> - `r'```(?:python\s+)?(.*?)```'`: This regex pattern can match both single-language-specified (e.g., `python`) and unspecified code fences.
>   - `(?:python\s+)?`: This is a non-capturing group (`(?:...)`). The `python\s+` part is optional due to `?`, meaning it matches "python" followed by one or more whitespace characters if it exists, but it's not required.
>   - `(.*?)`: This captures everything lazily between the backticks. `.*?` is a non-greedy match, ensuring it captures as little as possible between the backticks. The `()` around it is a capturing group that selects the text content.
>   - `re.DOTALL`: The flag allows the `.` in the `.*?` to match newline characters, which is essential for capturing multiline text.
> 
> This approach will extract and print just the "Some text." part from each matching block, ignoring the backticks and any optional "python" markers.
